{"author":{"id":"1e0a6ff3e01313072cf7b3803c158c22d01a37717237fe198e814e781752b172"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1784646302,"nonce":"8ymxSG3EwjWgWs8PLTl8b55N83A=","message":"Root-cause analysis (no fix proposed).\n\nThe send runs **synchronously inside `with self.suspend():`** on a Textual\nevent-loop callback: `action_send` -\u003e `_on_send_confirmed`\n(`src/b4/review_tui/_review_app.py:1633-1668`) enters `self.suspend()` at\nline 1637 and calls `b4.send_mail(..., patatt_sign=...)`.\n\nSigning chain: `send_mail` -\u003e `patatt.rfc2822_sign()`\n(`src/b4/__init__.py:5031`) -\u003e patatt `_sign_openpgp` runs\n`gpg --batch ... -s -u \u003ckey\u003e`. With a bad/revoked key, gpg exits 2 (matches\nLorenzo's strace), patatt raises `SigningError`, and `send_mail` re-raises it\nas `RuntimeError` (`src/b4/__init__.py:5039`).\n\nWhy it freezes instead of showing an error: the exception propagates out of\nthe body while the driver is suspended. The `except Exception` at\n`_review_app.py:1667` does catch it and call `self.notify('Send failed: ...')`,\nbut the notification never becomes visible -- the app does not come back from\nthe suspended state, leaving the \"idle event loop\" Lorenzo observed. `--no-sign`\navoids it (no gpg call); fixing the key avoids it (signing succeeds and\nsuspend() resumes normally).\n\nThe same synchronous-signing-inside-suspend() pattern is also in\n`_send_followup_reply` (`_review_app.py:1730`). The Patchwork path was already\nmoved off `suspend()` for this class of problem in commit c3504dd (\"pw: track\nseries via a quiet worker instead of suspending\"); the review send/reply paths\nwere not.","files":null}]}